// ★★ THE QUERY BIND — a request, a private return channel, and a reply, in ONE LINE.
//
// `!?` is Rholang's request/response sugar. Written at the call site it is one row of a `for`:
//
//     for(price <- @"quote"!?(14)) { … }
//
// and it means, exactly:
//
//     new r in { @"quote"!(*r, 14) | for(price <- r) { … } }
//
//   * `new r` MINTS a return channel nobody else can name;
//   * `@"quote"!(*r, 14)` SENDS the request — the return channel first, then the arguments;
//   * `for(price <- r)` RECEIVES the reply on that private channel.
//
// The send is the leg that matters. Until 2026-07-28 the expansion was computed and then
// THROWN AWAY, so the program that ran still held the raw query row — which resembles an
// ordinary receive closely enough that the lowering read `@"quote"` as the receive channel,
// dropped the arguments, and emitted NO REQUEST SEND AT ALL. The receive then waited forever
// on a channel nobody sends to.
//
//
// ★ WHY THIS FILE CARRIES THREE ROWS AND NOT ONE
// ──────────────────────────────────────────────
// A receive with no partner is SUPPOSED to rest. So the defect raised nothing, printed
// nothing, and exited zero — byte-identical to a correct program whose service happens not to
// reply. Silence is not evidence. Three rows run here, in one process, so that each reading is
// refuted by something else in the same run:
//
//   row                        publishes            what its outcome rules out
//   ─────────────────────────  ───────────────────  ──────────────────────────────────────────
//   the CONTROL (ordinary      "the desk is open"   "the harness cannot see @"OUT" at all"
//     send/receive pair)
//   the QUERY (`!?`)           42                   "the round trip did not happen"
//   the CLOSED DESK (`!?` to   nothing              "any `!?` row publishes unconditionally"
//     a service that is absent)
//
// Read the outcome as a TRIPLE. Two observations and no third is the pass; one observation is
// a broken round trip; three observations would mean a query answered itself.
//
//
// ★ 42 IS COMPUTED, NOT TRANSCRIBED
// ─────────────────────────────────
// Search this file for `42`: it appears in this comment and nowhere in the program. The desk
// receives `14` and replies `cents * 3`; the reducer performs the multiplication on the
// f1r3node machine and 42 is what rests on @"OUT". Change the `14` and the answer moves.
//
// ★ THE DESK NEVER PUBLISHES TO @"OUT" ITSELF — it replies on whatever return channel arrived.
// So an observation on @"OUT" is reachable ONLY through the complete round trip: the request
// send, the reply on the private channel, and the query row's own receive firing. A desk that
// published directly would look identical whether the return channel worked or not.
//
// ★ THE DESK PINS THE ARGUMENT BY PATTERN — `@cents` is the second field of the request, so a
// reply also witnesses that the argument travelled. An expansion that forwarded only the
// return channel would leave this desk resting forever.
//
// EXPECT: TWO observations — the control's greeting, and 42. The closed desk publishes
// nothing, and that is the third fact.

@"open"!("the desk is open") |

for(@greeting <- @"open") {

  @"OUT"!(greeting) |

  for(@reply, @cents <- @"quote") { @reply!(cents * 3) } |

  for(price <- @"quote"!?(14)) { @"OUT"!(*price) } |

  for(unanswered <- @"closed"!?(14)) { @"OUT"!("A CLOSED DESK ANSWERED") }
}
